      "content": "Iron (Fe) behaves differently from manganese (Mn) as soil pH increases. At more acidic texts (pH below 5.5), iron is highly soluble and forms Fe^2+ ions, making it readily available for vineyard roots. However, Fe^2+ ions are rapidly oxidized to less soluble Fe^3+ forms as the pH elevates, especially at the higher end of the range, around 7.0. Once it is oxidized to Fe^3+, iron precipitates with various soil components to form iron oxides, resulting in lowered bioavailability to grapevine roots. This ph fluctuation's impact on iron solubility is less extreme than that on manganese because iron is less prone to such rapid and total insolubility shifts in comparison to manganese. Iron chelates, such as ferrous sulfate, are sometimes applied to ensure iron remains available to grapevines, highlighting the contrast between Mn and Fe availability with pH adjustments. Essentially, Fe ions will transition from soluble forms that grapevine roots can absorb when the soil is acidic, to forms that are less soluble and much less available as the pH approaches neutral (>6.5), potentially leading to iron chlorosis in plants where deficiency symptoms",
